WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.222
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.522 --> 00:00:09.315
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.615 --> 00:00:16.145
Unit fourteen: Comparative Literature. Today you will question assumptions with rhetorical and embedded questions.

4
00:00:16.445 --> 00:00:19.005
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:23.200
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.527
Comparative literature.

7
00:00:26.517 --> 00:00:30.792
Comparative literature means the study of literature across languages and cultures.

8
00:00:31.383 --> 00:00:35.206
Comparative literature reads Khmer and French novels side by side.

9
00:00:35.817 --> 00:00:38.957
Your turn. Say: Comparative literature.

10
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.556
Comparative literature.

11
00:00:44.317 --> 00:00:45.643
World literature.

12
00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:50.832
World literature means literature that circulates beyond its culture of origin.

13
00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:54.136
The Reamker deserves a place in world literature.

14
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.309
Your turn. Say: World literature.

15
00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:01.667
World literature.

16
00:01:02.417 --> 00:01:03.116
Canon.

17
00:01:04.117 --> 00:01:07.254
Canon means the body of works considered most important.

18
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:10.616
The canon has long excluded women writers.

19
00:01:11.217 --> 00:01:13.718
Your turn. Say: Canon.

20
00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:17.324
Canon.

21
00:01:18.083 --> 00:01:19.282
Translation.

22
00:01:20.283 --> 00:01:23.351
Translation means rendering a text into another language.

23
00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.112
Translation allows works to travel.

24
00:01:26.717 --> 00:01:29.288
Your turn. Say: Translation.

25
00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:33.320
Translation.

26
00:01:34.083 --> 00:01:35.270
Influence.

27
00:01:36.283 --> 00:01:40.059
Influence means the effect one writer or tradition has on another.

28
00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:44.008
Indian epics had a strong influence on Khmer literature.

29
00:01:44.617 --> 00:01:47.455
Your turn. Say: Influence.

30
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:51.568
Influence.

31
00:01:52.317 --> 00:01:53.295
Tradition.

32
00:01:54.283 --> 00:01:58.303
Tradition means a body of practices passed down over generations.

33
00:01:58.917 --> 00:02:01.880
Oral tradition preserved many ancient stories.

34
00:02:02.483 --> 00:02:05.112
Your turn. Say: Tradition.

35
00:02:07.967 --> 00:02:08.759
Tradition.

36
00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:10.866
Cross-cultural.

37
00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.836
Cross-cultural means involving two or more cultures.

38
00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.984
The course takes a cross-cultural approach.

39
00:02:18.583 --> 00:02:21.410
Your turn. Say: Cross-cultural.

40
00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:25.628
Cross-cultural.

41
00:02:26.383 --> 00:02:27.419
Dialogue.

42
00:02:28.417 --> 00:02:32.355
Dialogue means an exchange of ideas between people or texts.

43
00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:35.426
The two novels are in dialogue with each other.

44
00:02:36.017 --> 00:02:38.541
Your turn. Say: Dialogue.

45
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:42.355
Dialogue.

46
00:02:43.167 --> 00:02:46.968
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.817 --> 00:02:52.338
Visal, you compared the Reamker with the Indian Ramayana. What did you discover?

48
00:02:53.450 --> 00:03:03.730
That influence is never one-way copying. The Khmer version transforms the story, so I began asking who decides which version is 'original'.

49
00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:07.687
And why isn't the Reamker better known internationally?

50
00:03:08.783 --> 00:03:16.065
That raises a bigger question: who decides which works belong to the canon, and whose languages get translated?

51
00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.312
So you're questioning the canon itself.

52
00:03:20.417 --> 00:03:27.829
Yes. If we only read European classics, how can we claim to understand world literature at all?

53
00:03:28.967 --> 00:03:30.362
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:31.217 --> 00:03:34.726
Grammar focus: Questioning rhetorical structures.

55
00:03:35.026 --> 00:03:36.294
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:39.296
Who decides which works belong to the canon?

57
00:03:40.183 --> 00:03:42.879
How can we claim to understand world literature?

58
00:03:43.783 --> 00:03:46.398
The question is whose languages get translated.

59
00:03:47.333 --> 00:03:59.613
Rhetorical questions challenge assumptions without expecting an answer. When 'who' is the subject, use no auxiliary ('Who decides?'). Embedded questions use statement order: 'the question is whose languages get translated'.

60
00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:04.825
Be careful! Don't say: Who decide which works belong to the canon?

61
00:04:06.767 --> 00:04:09.869
Say: Who decides which works belong to the canon?

62
00:04:10.169 --> 00:04:12.354
Subject 'who' takes a singular verb.

63
00:04:13.167 --> 00:04:15.761
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:16.567 --> 00:04:18.241
Reading Across Borders.

65
00:04:18.541 --> 00:04:23.757
Comparative literature resists the idea that national literatures can be understood in isolation.

66
00:04:24.767 --> 00:04:29.564
Stories have always travelled, carried by traders, monks and, later, translators.

67
00:04:30.567 --> 00:04:35.817
Yet the question of which texts circulate globally is shaped by economic and political power.

68
00:04:36.833 --> 00:04:42.211
Why, for instance, are English novels translated into Khmer far more often than the reverse?

69
00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.578
By placing traditions in dialogue, comparatists ask whose stories have been left out of the canon.

70
00:04:49.667 --> 00:04:53.576
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:54.417 --> 00:04:56.103
Choose the correct question.

72
00:04:56.403 --> 00:05:02.146
A, Who does decide the canon?. B, Who decides the canon?. C, Who decide the canon?.

73
00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.454
The answer is B: Who decides the canon?.

74
00:05:09.617 --> 00:05:11.674
Choose the correct embedded question.

75
00:05:11.974 --> 00:05:18.716
A, I wonder whose stories get told.. B, I wonder whose stories do get told?. C, I wonder whose do stories get told..

76
00:05:22.267 --> 00:05:25.346
The answer is A: I wonder whose stories get told..

77
00:05:26.483 --> 00:05:29.911
The body of works considered most important is the blank.

78
00:05:30.211 --> 00:05:33.830
A, canon. B, tradition. C, dialogue.

79
00:05:37.367 --> 00:05:39.006
The answer is A: canon.

80
00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:42.057
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:05:42.357 --> 00:05:48.506
A, exert an influence on. B, make an influence to. C, do an influence for.

82
00:05:52.067 --> 00:05:54.786
The answer is A: exert an influence on.

83
00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:59.668
According to the reading, what shapes which texts circulate globally?

84
00:05:59.968 --> 00:06:05.583
A, the length of texts. B, economic and political power. C, readers' age.

85
00:06:09.133 --> 00:06:12.387
The answer is B: economic and political power.

86
00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:17.204
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:21.396
Why might the literary 'canon' be worth questioning or expanding?

88
00:06:38.183 --> 00:06:39.684
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:39.984 --> 00:07:15.616
The literary canon is worth questioning because it reflects who held power when it was formed, not only which works are best. For centuries, it mainly included male European writers, so many brilliant voices were ignored. For example, few international courses include Khmer or other Southeast Asian classics. Some argue that a canon must be selective to be useful. I agree, but I believe the selection should be revised regularly so that it represents the whole world.

90
00:07:15.916 --> 00:07:17.544
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:23.181
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and questioning rhetorical structures.

92
00:07:23.481 --> 00:07:28.838
Today's value is cultural openness. Read beyond your own tradition to see the world more fully.

93
00:07:29.138 --> 00:07:31.779
See you in Unit fifteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:32.079 --> 00:07:34.894
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

